june's jobs report shows employers added  850 000 jobs last month which is up from  583  000 in may and may's unemployment rate  of 5.8  ticked up to 5.9 for june  last month's jobs gain leaves us still  millions  of drops short of where we were right  before the pandemic hit  at the same time the number of job  openings has grown really quickly  in recent months and the number of  unemployed people  nearly matches the number of job  openings which  raises the question why aren't those  unemployed people  filling those job openings right now a  lot of people  are vaccinated they're going out again  they're traveling  eating at restaurants and so businesses  need to hire to meet that demand  however a lot of unemployed people or  people without work  are not even looking for a job right now  we've seen that the number of job  openings are growing  in hospitality recreation and food  service industries but many workers who  filled these jobs  could still be reluctant or unable to  return to these types of jobs  some have health concerns still about  the coronavirus  in other cases you have parents who have  to stay at home  with their kids because schools and day  cares are still at least partially shut  down  and then others might be holding out  looking for work because of expanded  unemployment benefits  federal reserve chairman jerome powell  says that there's another reason  the very quick job gains of the early  recovery  essentially involved going back to your  old job that's that's not so much what's  happening now now it's actually finding  new jobs and  that's a that's a matching function that  that is more labor-intensive and  time-consuming and there may be a bit of  a speed limit on that  employers now have to basically rebuild  their workforces from scratch  so their employees that they laid off  last summer they might have moved to a  different city for instance  and now that employer has to find  people who are nearby june's numbers  show that the percentage of americans  working or  actively looking for work held steady at  61.6  in june which tells us that there are  still a lot of americans who have yet to  return to the job market  labor force participation fell really  sharply at the beginning of the pandemic  and has had a very slow recovery in some  cases people are just  discouraged their industries might not  be coming back very quickly and so  they're not looking for jobs right now  average hourly earnings grew really  swiftly last month  and this is a sign that employers are  ramping up  wages as they have to attract workers in  this really competitive market  and there could be implications for  inflation because  if companies have to increase wages they  also might have to  pass along price increases to customers  they're doing things like offering  bonuses and pay raises  and this puts workers in a really strong  position they have a lot of bargaining  power and we expect this to continue for  the next  at least few months
 

